<h2>How the appeal was lost — twice</h2>
<p>The appeal had already been dismissed once, on 2 September 2026, when it was called and the respondent&rsquo;s side did not appear. The recall application was the attempt to undo that. Its foundation was a single assertion: counsel were engaged before other Benches at the relevant time.</p>
<p>The Division Bench tested the assertion against its own record. The order of 2 September recorded the absence; it recorded nothing about the reason, because no reason had been communicated to the court that day. The explanation of engagement elsewhere came from outside the record — &ldquo;information from other sources,&rdquo; as the Bench put it. And the surrounding history made the assertion harder, not easier, to accept: the applicant had changed its advocate-on-record, listings had been intimated by SMS and telephone on several dates, and the matter had already consumed multiple adjournments. The conclusion was terse: &ldquo;the explanation offered is not sufficient.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>The merits that never got heard</h2>
<p>The discipline of the order lies in what it refused to examine. The applicant had substantive arguments waiting — that the trial court&rsquo;s order in the Section 34 proceeding was not truly a &ldquo;modification&rdquo; of the arbitral award, and that Section 31(7)(b) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act permitted what was done. The Bench expressly declined to decide them. A recall application puts one question in issue: was there sufficient cause for the absence? The strength of the absent party&rsquo;s case is not a substitute for an answer.</p>
<div class="note">The logic parallels restoration practice everywhere, including Delhi: restoration and recall are indulgences granted on cause shown, and the cause must appear from material the court can verify — an affidavit of the engaged counsel, the cause lists of the conflicting matters, a contemporaneous letter to the Bench officer. Assertion without anchorage fails.</div>

<h2>Building a recall application that survives</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Fix the record on the day itself.</strong> If a conflict is known, have a proxy appear and seek a pass-over, or deliver a written request to the court officer. The contemporaneous trace is the whole case later.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Move immediately.</strong> Mention the dismissal the same day or the next morning. Promptness is itself evidence of good faith; delay compounds the insufficiency of the explanation.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Prove, do not assert.</strong> Annex the cause lists showing the conflicting engagement, the appearance slips, and an affidavit from the counsel concerned. The explanation must live inside the application&rsquo;s papers.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Account for the history.</strong> Where the matter has a record of adjournments or changes of advocate-on-record, address it candidly. Courts read recall applications against the file, and silence about the history reads as evasion.</div>
</div>

<h2>The client-care dimension</h2>
<p>Orders like this one are ultimately about the client, who loses an appeal without anyone deciding it. Litigants engaging counsel in high-volume commercial lists should insist on written listing-tracking protocols and named fallback counsel. And where a dismissal in absence does occur, the honest advice is the one this order enforces: the recall window is narrow, evidentiary and unforgiving — and the alternative route, a challenge to the main order itself, must be evaluated at once before limitation runs.</p>
